Headwind is the wind component along the runway, so it uses cosine where crosswind (X10) uses sine. To stay consistent with X10, I mirrored its structure exactly, including the existing DEGREES call.

{"formula": "=X8*COS(DEGREES(U7-X7))"}

Note: X10 converts the angle with DEGREES, but SIN and COS expect radians, so RADIANS is probably what was intended in both cells.

B37 most likely mirrors the Column C block. There, B27 finds the largest value below the last entry, using `=MAX(IF(B9:C20<B23,B9:C20))`. The Column D equivalent, comparing against the last D value in B32, is below.

This is a best guess from that pattern. Another possibility is that B37 looks up the date for B36, the same way B28 does for Column C.

{"formula": "=MAX(IF(D9:D20<B32,D9:D20))"}

Column N already uses `=ABS(E2-K2)` for Cost Delta, so I'm guessing Time Delta follows the same pattern with the time columns (D and J).

One caveat: D2 and J2 look like they hold text such as "Jan 5 2012 12:15:32.12", not true Excel date/time values. If Excel can't convert that text, this formula will return `#VALUE!`. In that case the text would need to be parsed into a real date/time first.

{"formula": "=ABS(D2-J2)"}

The user is in the "Total Value" column (M4), next to the stock code in K4. The most likely intent is to total the "Total" column (F) for that stock, limited to trades between the From Date in H4 and the To Date in I4.

The dates are anchored with `$` because the date range only seems to be entered in H4/I4. I can't tell whether sells are stored as negative values, so the formula simply adds column F as it is.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($F$4:$F$36,$B$4:$B$36,K4,$A$4:$A$36,\">=\"&$H$4,$A$4:$A$36,\"<=\"&$I$4)"}

Column I holds Accrued Interest. Excel's ACCRINT function calculates it from the issue date (E2), the first interest date, the settlement date (D2), the coupon rate (H2/100), the par value (B2), the frequency (G2) and the basis (1).

The row already uses COUPNCD, COUPPCD, YIELD and PRICE with basis 1, so the accrued interest from the last coupon date to the buy date is a simpler fit. That gives B2 × (H2/100) / G2 × the fraction of the coupon period elapsed. With basis 1, that fraction is (D2−M2)/(L2−M2), using the last (M2) and next (L2) interest payment dates.

{"formula": "=B2*H2/100/G2*(D2-M2)/(L2-M2)"}

The worksheet looks like a fiscal calendar: B2:B54 holds week numbers, C1:G1 holds what appear to be years, and C2:G54 holds the start date of each week. Assuming C1:G1 are years, J4 should find the column for the year of the date in J3. It then returns the week whose start date is the last one on or before that date.

If C1:G1 are not years, a simpler alternative is `=WEEKNUM(J3)`.

{"formula": "=XLOOKUP(J3,INDEX(C2:G54,0,XMATCH(YEAR(J3),C1:G1)),B2:B54,,-1)"}

The refund rows next to this cell already find their rate in D33 with `AGGREGATE(15,6,...)` against the rate table. This cell is the "Fixed Deduction per Table" column, so the formula below applies the same lookup to the Fixed Deduction column (C3:C20) instead of the rate column.

It returns the smallest fixed deduction among the table rows whose rounded "Exceeds" value (column A) is at least the amount in A33. The table references are made absolute (`$`) so the formula can be filled down to B34:B35. Those absolute references are my assumption, since the context does not show D33's exact references.

{"formula": "=AGGREGATE(15,6,$C$3:$C$20/(ROUND($A$3:$A$20,0)>=A33),1)"}

H1 is labelled "1st Product wt highest cost". The column next to it, F, already holds a TRUE/FALSE test for "is this the highest cost" (`=B2=MAX($B$2:$B$7)`). So H2 is most likely the matching test for "is this the first row that has the highest cost".

That same test appears inside the formulas in column I and column L. It counts how many rows so far have the maximum cost and checks that this row is one of them.

{"formula": "=AND(COUNTIF($B$2:$B2,MAX($B$2:$B$7))=1,B2=MAX($B$2:$B$7))"}

F47 holds Theta (Θ) for the State Plane to Coordinates direction. F46 already computes x/y as `(B47-D8)/(D11-B48)`, so Theta is the arctangent of that ratio.

The result is in radians, which matches how it is used later: F48 converts it with `F47*360/(2*PI())`, and F52 and F67 apply COS to it directly.

{"formula": "=ATAN(F46)"}

Column D is labeled "Percentage by event", so I read D14 as the average of the "Very satisfied" percentages across the five events (B3:F3). Each of those cells holds text like "9, 64.3%". The formula pulls out the part after the comma and converts it to a number.

D3 appears to be empty, so its conversion fails. IFERROR turns that failure into blank text, which AVERAGE skips. The result is the average of the events that have data.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(IFERROR(VALUE(MID(B3:F3,FIND(\",\",B3:F3)+2,10)),\"\"))"}

T7 sits under "Avg. Positions held", so it most likely averages the position counts in AC for the year in G7. The formula below filters the AB dates (AB7:AB29) to that calendar year and averages the matching AC values.

I didn't use a simpler `(J7+P7)/2`, because J can contain "-" and that would cause an error.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIFS($AC$7:$AC$29,$AB$7:$AB$29,\">=\"&DATE(G7,1,1),$AB$7:$AB$29,\"<=\"&DATE(G7,12,31))"}

The "Average" column most likely averages the 16 trial values (B4:Q4) in the same row, matching the range the IQR formula in column R uses.

The ".5 Below" and ".5 Above" columns suggest the user might instead want an average that excludes outliers outside those bounds. Since the exact bound logic isn't clear, a plain average is the safest prediction.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(B4:Q4)"}

Row 24 holds shift start times (B24:J24) and A25 is "A", which matches employee "A" in row 4. So B25 most likely counts how many of A's shifts across B4:CN4 begin at the time in B24. Shift cells hold text like "07:00-15:00", so the formula turns B24 into "hh:mm" text and matches with a trailing wildcard.

The row reference is left relative so that copying down maps row 26 to row 5 (employee B), and so on.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF($B4:$CN4,TEXT(B$24,\"hh:mm\")&\"*\")"}

The headers show N7 = "Hours", O7 = "No. of transaction" and P7 = "Total DT". P8 already uses SUMIFS to total downtime hours for each one-hour window starting at N8. O8 most likely counts the downtime entries in that same window, so I used COUNTIFS with the same time criteria.

The existing pattern hides whether the references in P8 are absolute. I assumed they lock the data range G2:G28 so the formula can be filled down.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($G$2:$G$28,\">=\"&N8,$G$2:$G$28,\"<\"&TIME(HOUR(N8),MINUTE(N8)+60,0))"}

L1 is "Next Interest Payment Date", which is the next coupon date after settlement. Excel's COUPNCD(settlement, maturity, frequency, basis) returns exactly that.

Here settlement is the buy date (D2), maturity is F2, and frequency is G2. The ACCRINT formula in column I uses basis 1, so I'll use basis 1 here too for consistency.

{"formula": "=COUPNCD(D2,F2,G2,1)"}

D2 sits between the Month and Time columns, so it most likely holds the day number pulled from A2.

In "Fri 14 Mar 7:50pm", the day starts at position 5, so `MID(A2,5,2)` returns "14". `TRIM` handles single-digit days like "1 ", and `--` turns the text into a number.

{"formula": "=--TRIM(MID(A2,5,2))"}

The "month payed" column most likely looks up which pay period the completed date in I3 falls into. Each pay period starts on the date in column B and has its month in column A.

`LOOKUP` finds the last "In (from)" date that is on or before I3 and returns that row's month from A. This assumes the dates in B3:B41 are in ascending order. The `IF` returns a blank when I3 is empty, because column I only has dates down to row 16.

{"formula": "=IF(I3=\"\",\"\",LOOKUP(I3,$B$3:$B$41,$A$3:$A$41))"}

Cell H6 is "Total Vacation Days (since start)," and G10 calculates remaining days as `=H6-G8`, which subtracts only the 2017 days taken. That suggests H6 adds the 2016 carry-over balance (H5) to the days earned since the start date. This mirrors the sick-hours formula in Q6, which counts months with `DATEDIF` and multiplies by a monthly rate.

The vacation accrual rate isn't stated anywhere in the sheet. The 1.25 days per month (15 days a year) is my guess, so replace it with your actual rate.

{"formula": "=H5+DATEDIF(G3,TODAY(),\"M\")*(1.25)"}

The Completed Date values are stored as text with a weekday in front, such as "Friday, April 09, 2010 5:34…". The formula finds the first comma and keeps everything after it, so "April 09, 2010 5:34…" is left. `VALUE` then turns that into a date-time serial. This conversion assumes a US date setting.

Some rows hold notes instead of dates, such as B5 ("No need to take any action"). For those, `IFERROR` returns a blank instead of an error.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(VALUE(MID(B2,FIND(\",\",B2)+2,100)),\"\")"}

G3 sits under the "GCD" header, and H3 and I3 divide C3 and D3 by G3 to build the ratio in J3. So G3 should hold the greatest common divisor of the Students and Managers values in that row.

{"formula": "=GCD(C3,D3)"}

Note: `GCD` drops decimals before calculating. With non-whole values like 913.32, it will often return 1, so the ratio in J3 won't be simplified.

The intent for H13 (Net Turbine Rating, MW) isn't clear enough to predict a formula, so I'm returning an empty string.

The obvious guess would be total net output divided by the number of turbines (`=N12/H12`). However, Site Condition (MW) in N10 is built from a product of H-column cells, most likely `=H12*H13`. N12 sums N10:N11, so `=N12/H12` would probably create a circular reference. That suggests H13 is normally a typed-in rating rather than a formula, and nothing else on the sheet points to what it should be.

{"formula": ""}

The pay plan seems to be laid out in tiers: rows 2–3 look like the lower and upper show-count limits, and row 4 the pay rate for each tier. F11 holds the number of shows. So "Total Show $" in H11 is probably the show count times the rate for the tier that count falls into. The formula finds that rate with `LOOKUP` against the lower limits in C2:I2.

This assumes the row 4 amounts are paid per show. If each tier instead pays a flat amount, drop the `F11*`.

{"formula": "=F11*LOOKUP(F11,$C$2:$I$2,$C$4:$I$4)"}

Column O is "Night hours worked", and the night window is set in N2 (start) and O2 (end). Columns I and J already add a day to times before the night end, so a shift that crosses midnight runs in one straight line.

The formula measures how much of the shift (I6 to J6) overlaps the night window. That window runs from N2 to O2 plus one day, since the night ends the next morning. If the shift does not touch the night window, the overlap is negative, so `MAX(0,…)` returns zero instead.

{"formula": "=MAX(0,MIN(J6,$O$2+1)-MAX(I6,$N$2))"}

Column E is "Days late", so the formula subtracts the Target Date (B4) from the Submitted date (C4). `MAX(0, …)` keeps early submissions at 0 instead of showing a negative number.

This is a best guess. The "Incorrect - Should be 2" and "Should be 5" notes in column F hint that some rows may need a different calculation. One possibility is counting only working days, e.g. `NETWORKDAYS(B4,C4)-1`, which I can't confirm from the data shown.

{"formula": "=MAX(0,C4-B4)"}

Cell B10 is labeled "Bearing (DMS)", so it should convert the decimal bearing in B9 into degrees, minutes and seconds.

The formula takes the whole degrees with INT(B9), the whole minutes with INT((B9-INT(B9))*60), and the seconds with ROUND(MOD(B9*3600,60),0), then joins them with the °, ' and " symbols.

{"formula": "=INT(B9)&\"° \"&INT((B9-INT(B9))*60)&\"' \"&ROUND(MOD(B9*3600,60),0)&\"\"\"\""}

B17 should hold the whole-years part of the average account age, to pair with C17's leftover months.

C17 computes the average age in months: `(SUM(B2:B7)*12+SUM(C2:C7))/ROWS(A2:A7)`, then takes `MOD(...,12)` to get the remaining months. So B17 uses the same average-months expression, divides by 12, and keeps only the whole number with `INT`.

{"formula": "=INT(((SUM(B2:B7)*12+SUM(C2:C7))/ROWS(A2:A7))/12)"}

Column C holds ages and column B holds birth dates, so C5 most likely calculates the age from B5. `DATEDIF` with `TODAY()` gives the age in whole years.

C2:C4 hold plain numbers rather than formulas, so there is no existing pattern to copy. The I and J columns calculate ages against the fixed year 2024, so `=2024-YEAR(B5)` is a possible alternative.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(B5,TODAY(),\"Y\")"}

M7 should return the largest savings for Wisconsin, the site named in J5 ("Wisconsin Top 10"). It's the first row of the top-10 list, so k = ROWS(M$7:M7), which equals 1 here and grows as the formula is copied down.

The site is taken from J5 with the same LEFT/FIND expression the J7:L7 formulas use. Those formulas then match each savings value back to its row, using COUNTIF to handle ties.

{"formula": "=LARGE(IF($B$4:$B$49=LEFT($J$5,FIND(\" \",$J$5)-1),$G$4:$G$49),ROWS(M$7:M7))"}

G5 most likely mirrors the "What I need" layout on the right. There, column O uses LARGE next to column N's SMALL formula. Shifting that pattern over to the left side (I→A, K→C) gives the LARGE counterpart for G5.

{"formula": "=LARGE(A4:D4,COUNTIF(A4:D4,\">\"&C5)+1)"}

Correction: the pattern references the same row, @ = 5, so the formula is:

{"formula": "=LARGE(A5:D5,COUNTIF(A5:D5,\">\"&C5)+1)"}

The shortage in J6 is matched to its bracket in A6:D14 using an approximate-match VLOOKUP. The result is that bracket's fixed deduction (column C) plus its rate (column D) applied to the amount above the bracket's lower limit (column A).

This assumes the "Exceeds" thresholds in A6:A14 are sorted ascending, which approximate matching requires.

{"formula": "=VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,3,TRUE)+VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,4,TRUE)*(J6-VLOOKUP(J6,$A$6:$D$14,1,TRUE))"}

The second table appears to use a Monday–Saturday office week (Sunday-only weekend), so I adapted the formula from F4. It uses `NETWORKDAYS.INTL` with weekend code `11`, which excludes Sundays only. It takes the office hours from J33:K33. It assumes the second holiday list sits in J40:J57, the same 18 rows as J11:J28 in the first table; the context doesn't show those dates, so adjust the range if needed.

One limitation: this applies the J33:K33 hours to Saturdays too. If row 34 (J34:K34) holds different Saturday hours, this formula won't account for them.

{"formula": "=MAX(0,SUM(MAX(0,$K$33-$J$33-MOD(C33-$J$33,1)),MIN($K$33-$J$33,MOD(E33-$J$33,1)),(NETWORKDAYS.INTL(B33+C33-$J$33,D33+E33-$J$33,11,$J$40:$J$57)-2)*($K$33-$J$33)))*24"}

Column L is labelled "BOTH / 2ND SCORE". It feeds M ("ELIGIBLE SCORES") as `SUM(J:L)`. J is the top WAGC score and K is the top CMT score. So L probably adds one more best result taken from either competition.

My best guess is the third-largest score across B4:I4. Taking the second-largest risked counting a top score already in J or K. This is an approximation: if the two top scores and the third-largest happen to fall differently between WAGC and CMT, it may not match the sheet's exact rule.

{"formula": "=LARGE(B4:I4,3)"}

The label in L10 reads "Latest patch common to all countries", so M10 should find the Model A date that every country has reached. Patching is cumulative, so that is the earliest of the per-country latest dates in M3:M8.

A plain `MIN` would break here. When a country has no Model A row, `MAXIFS` returns 0, and that 0 would become the minimum. Model A seems to be listed only for CAN, MEX and USA, which fits the expected 2024-12. Using `MINIFS` with `">0"` skips those empty countries.

{"formula": "=MINIFS(M3:M8,M3:M8,\">0\")"}

Cell E36 is "PV of total free cash flows", and the forecast free cash flows sit in E34:G34, so the formula most likely discounts those with NPV.

The discount rate is a guess: C36 pulls a value from the 'OAT Inputs' sheet, and I've assumed that value is the discount rate. If C36 holds something else, swap in the correct rate cell.

{"formula": "=NPV($C$36,E34:G34)"}

B11 is labeled "Total TWR" (A11). The monthly time-weighted returns are in F6:F8, so the total is found by chaining them: multiply (1 + each monthly return) together and subtract 1. This matches how "Total Return" in B10 is calculated as a ratio minus 1.

Note: `1+F6:F8` inside `PRODUCT` needs a version of Excel with dynamic arrays (Excel 365 or 2021). In older versions, enter it with Ctrl+Shift+Enter or use `=(1+F6)*(1+F7)*(1+F8)-1` instead.

{"formula": "=PRODUCT(1+F6:F8)-1"}

The label in A28 says "balloon defn 2 (last pmt)", so I read this as: the final payment is the balloon amount in B23. On that reading, B29 is the regular payment over the first B22−1 periods.

After those payments, the remaining balance must equal B23 discounted back one period, which is B23/(1+B16/12). That value is passed as PMT's future-value argument. The result is rounded up to the cent, matching B9 and B25.

{"formula": "=ROUNDUP(PMT(B16/12,B22-1,-B15,B23/(1+B16/12)),2)"}

Cell E16 labels this row "sd", directly under "mean" in D15. So D16 should hold the standard deviation of the choice #1 distribution, where A11:A37 are the values and C11:C37 are their counts.

The formula weights each squared deviation from the mean (D15) by its count, divides by the total count H2, and takes the square root. That divisor matches how D15 computes the mean. It gives the population standard deviation. If you want the sample version, divide by `(H2-1)` instead.

{"formula": "=SQRT(SUMPRODUCT(C11:C37,(A11:A37-D15)^2)/H2)"}

H17 should hold the "Sum returns" counterpart to G17 for the two-variable table. It follows the L17 pattern with one criteria pair removed, so it sums D where A matches the first letter of F17 and B matches the second.

The exact references in G17 aren't shown, only the pattern `A@:A@`. The other formulas cover rows 2:534, so I've assumed the same range, written as `$A$2:$A$534` to match the absolute style used in G3/H3.

{"formula": "=SUMIFS($D$2:$D$534,$A$2:$A$534,MID(F17,1,1),$B$2:$B$534,MID(F17,2,1))"}

P3 most likely needs the total quantity of the fruit in O3 between the two dates in P1 (start) and Q1 (end). The formula checks each product in C2:C29 against O3, keeps only the date columns in D1:M1 that fall from P1 to Q1, and adds up the matching quantities in D2:M29.

The sheet also has a country selector (T1/T3), but I couldn't tell which cell holds the chosen country, so this formula does not filter by country.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(($C$2:$C$29=O3)*($D$1:$M$1>=$P$1)*($D$1:$M$1<=$Q$1)*$D$2:$M$29)"}

W4 is in the SE (BP) column. The neighbouring X column (SE (CP)) uses `=SUMSQ(V@:V@)/B@`, which builds a running sum of squared absolute errors and divides by the period number in column B.

The BP version should follow the same pattern using the BP absolute errors in column U. Starting the range at U$4 makes it a running total as the formula is filled down.

{"formula": "=SUMSQ(U$4:U4)/B4"}

J2 holds the earliest date attended. The date columns are C, E, G and I, so the formula takes their minimum.

Cells where an activity is blank or not a date return "", and MIN skips text, so those are ignored.

{"formula": "=MIN(C2,E2,G2,I2)"}

Cell L13 is in the output table, which looks like each region's monthly sales converted using the FX table. The formula takes the matching sales figure (E13) and multiplies it by the FX rate found by looking up two things:
- the row's currency (K13) in the FX currency list (C4:C6);
- the column's month (L12) in the FX month headers (D3:G3).

The anchoring lets the formula be filled across and down the output table. The first argument (E13) is left relative so it shifts with the fill. The currency column ($K) and the month row (L$12) are partly locked so they keep pointing at the right labels.

{"formula": "=E13*INDEX($D$4:$G$6,MATCH($K13,$C$4:$C$6,0),MATCH(L$12,$D$3:$G$3,0))"}

L4 holds the "Person ID" header and M4 holds "Decision Date", with the ID value AAA4 in L5. So M5 should look up the decision date for that person.

The lookup matches L5 against the Person ID column (C3:C9) and returns the matching value from the Decision Date column (J3:J9). If no match is found, it shows "Not Found".

{"formula": "=XLOOKUP(L5,$C$3:$C$9,$J$3:$J$9,\"Not Found\")"}

The prediction below assumes D4 should follow the D2 pattern for Provider 2. In D2, only the first OFFSET shifts by provider row (`INT(ROW()-2)/2*11`, so 11 rows down for row 4); the other two use 0. I applied that row offset to all three OFFSET calls so the whole formula reads Provider 2's block.

I kept the lookup values pointing at `'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C10:$C12`, assuming those cells hold the PTO codes for every provider. If they are per-provider instead, these references would need to shift down too.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$10,INT(ROW()-2)/2*11,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C10)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$10,INT(ROW()-2)/2*11,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C11)*8+COUNTIF(OFFSET('PTO Tracker 2025'!$F$10,INT(ROW()-2)/2*11,(COLUMN(A$1)-1)*8,6,7),'PTO Tracker 2025'!$C12)*4"}

The intent here is unclear, so this is a guess. C10 holds the hard-coded text "Positive Trend Day 1". D10 most likely continues that count, the same way row 7 does. The formula below adds 1 to the day number in C10 when D5 matches C5, and otherwise starts a new count at Day 1.

It will not fully match the "DESIRED RESULT" row. That row keeps a positive trend going through Unchanged days and some one-day reversals, and the sheet doesn't show the rule for that.

{"formula": "=IF(D5=C5,\"Trend \"&D5&\" Day \"&RIGHT(SUBSTITUTE(C10,\" \",\"     \"),3)+1,D5&\" Day 1\")"}

Columns B:F appear to hold the student IDs enrolled in each course, and K3:K32 holds the full student ID list. Rows 3 onward in Q, R and S each name three courses, so T3 likely counts the students enrolled in all three.

The formula finds each course's column with `MATCH` against the headers in B2:F2. It then uses `COUNTIF` to check, for every ID in K3:K32, whether that ID appears in that course's column. `SUMPRODUCT` multiplies the three checks together, so it counts only the students present in all three courses.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT((COUNTIF(INDEX($B$3:$F$24,0,MATCH(Q3,$B$2:$F$2,0)),$K$3:$K$32)>0)*(COUNTIF(INDEX($B$3:$F$24,0,MATCH(R3,$B$2:$F$2,0)),$K$3:$K$32)>0)*(COUNTIF(INDEX($B$3:$F$24,0,MATCH(S3,$B$2:$F$2,0)),$K$3:$K$32)>0))"}

This assumes V24 should count the trades whose time in E6:E203 falls between the start time in T23 and the end time in T24 (the "TIME RANGE" block next to "TRADES").

The filter values in T25:T29 (Congested, MACD, Momentum, 15M Confirmation, Retrace in Pips) are not included. If you want them applied too, more criteria can be added to the COUNTIFS.

{"formula": "=COUNTIFS($E$6:$E$203,\">=\"&$T$23,$E$6:$E$203,\"<=\"&$T$24)"}

C2 sits next to the word list. The formulas in B1:B2 check whether a word in column A needs more letter tiles than are available in H. My guess is that C2 should show how many blank tiles each word needs. The B formulas compare exactly that number against the blank count in H27.

This is an inference, because C1 has no formula to confirm the pattern. The ranges `H$1:H$26` and `ROW($65:$90)` are anchored because they must stay fixed for every word row, while `A2` stays relative.

{"formula": "=(SUM(ABS(H$1:H$26-LEN(A2)+LEN(SUBSTITUTE(A2,CHAR(ROW($65:$90)),""))))-SUM(H$1:H$26)+LEN(A2))/2"}

D2 most likely holds a cell address as text, because B2 and C2 pass `$D$2` to `INDIRECT`. Those formulas shift right from that address, and A2:C2 shows "A", "AA", "AAA". That matches the row in Table 2 (columns O:Q) where column O equals A2.

So D2 probably looks up A2 in column O and returns that cell's address, e.g. `$O$2`. I'm inferring this from the existing formulas rather than from anything explicit in the sheet.

{"formula": "=ADDRESS(MATCH(A2,$O:$O,0),COLUMN($O:$O))"}

Cell B4 sits under the "Client" header. The formulas in C4 and D4 look up B4 in column A of each product sheet ('Product A'!$A$3:$A$11), so B4 most likely pulls the first client name from that list.

This is a best guess. The I4:I6 labels ("Clnts", "Prod", "Rnge") suggest B4 could instead be a dynamic lookup driven by those cells, which I can't confirm from the context.

{"formula": "='Product A'!A3"}

Columns C and D hold numeric values X and Y, and E4/F4 are person columns, so the likely intent is a per-row calculation from those two values. The column B groups (A, B, C) are labels and probably don't drive the formula.

The simplest guess is that each person's share combines X and Y, so I'm proposing the product of the two values for this row. This is a low-confidence guess; it could just as easily be a sum or difference.

{"formula": "=C5*D5"}

The heading in E1 reads "COUNT NON DUPLICATES IN COLUM…", and E2:E11 holds dates, so this most likely counts the distinct values in column E. `SUMPRODUCT(1/COUNTIF(...))` gives each repeated value a total weight of 1, so the sum equals the number of distinct values.

The heading is cut off and the dates continue through column I. If you meant all of E2:I11 rather than just column E, swap in that range.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(1/COUNTIF(E2:E11,E2:E11))"}

The formulas in D2 and E2 are the same formula copied across, with relative references shifting one column each time. Shifting D2 back one column gives the original for C2. That means `D2→C2`, `B2→A2`, `E2:F2→D2:E2` and `D2:$E2→C2:$E2`.

In C2, `COLUMNS($C2:C2)=1` is true, so the formula removes the trailing numbers from A2 and leaves the name.

{"formula": "=IF(COLUMNS($C2:C2)=1,SUBSTITUTE(A2,TRIM(RIGHT(SUBSTITUTE($A2,\" \",REPT(\" \",99)),99*COUNT(D2:E2))),),IFERROR(--LEFT(RIGHT(SUBSTITUTE($A2,\" \",REPT(\" \",99)),99*COLUMNS(C2:$E2)),99),\"\"))"}

This follows the counting formula already used in B14 and B22, adjusted for row 8. The `A@:A@` placeholders in that pattern hide the exact anchoring, so `A$1:A7` is my best guess.

The formula finds the nearest "Service" heading above in column A. It then counts how many cells in column B, from just below that heading down to B7, contain that service name.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(INDEX(B:B,LOOKUP(2,1/ISNUMBER(SEARCH(\"Service\",A$1:A7)),ROW(A$1:A7))+1):B7,LOOKUP(2,1/ISNUMBER(SEARCH(\"Service\",A$1:A7)),A$1:A7))"}

I'm assuming AC6 should calculate the moving average described in the AC5 header ("N Period avg - Apps to interviews"), with the number of periods set in AD4. This is a best guess about your intent.

The formula sums interviews booked (column R) and divides by the sum of applications (column Q). Both sums cover the last AD4 filled periods in rows 11–31, found with COUNT and OFFSET.

{"formula": "=IFERROR(SUM(OFFSET(R11,COUNT(R11:R31)-$AD$4,0,$AD$4,1))/SUM(OFFSET(Q11,COUNT(Q11:Q31)-$AD$4,0,$AD$4,1)),\"\")"}

The cells in L4:M14 look like a lookup that links each product to the crops it is used on. Customer A's field counts per crop sit in E4:J4. This formula takes the field count for the crop in this column (Corn). It multiplies that count by whether the customer's product (P4) is listed for that crop. The result is a "Use" figure that the Percent block below (Q15 onward) then divides by the row total.

This is a best guess from the layout, since the sheet's intent isn't stated outright.

{"formula": "=E4*COUNTIFS($L$4:$L$14,$P4,$M$4:$M$14,Q$3)"}

This formula guesses that "Longest delay" means the longest run of consecutive draws in which the 8-ball match did not occur. That meaning is inferred from the header and from K2 and M2; the worksheet does not state it directly.

It uses the standard longest-streak pattern. FREQUENCY counts the draws that are not 8 (`a!R<>8`) between each pair of rows where `a!R=8`, and MAX returns the largest of those gaps. It uses the same `a!` sheet and row range (2 to 2100) as K2.

{"formula": "=MAX(FREQUENCY(IF(a!$R$2:$R$2100<>8,ROW(a!$R$2:$R$2100)),IF(a!$R$2:$R$2100=8,ROW(a!$R$2:$R$2100))))"}

F3 most likely returns the easting of the old point closest to the new point in B3/C3. E3 and G3 both look up `$F3` in column L, so F3 must hold one of the column L eastings.

The formula finds the smallest squared distance between (B3, C3) and every old point in L3:M21. It then returns the easting from the row with that smallest distance. Comparing squared distances picks the same point as comparing real distances, so no square root is needed. In older Excel versions this may need to be entered as an array formula (Ctrl+Shift+Enter).

{"formula": "=INDEX($L$3:$L$21,MATCH(MIN(($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2),($L$3:$L$21-$B3)^2+($M$3:$M$21-$C3)^2,0))"}

