Cells B18:M18 hold totals like "13/15", and the cells above them hold text fractions, so N18 most likely totals the "x/y" values in N13:N17. This formula adds up the numerators and the denominators separately, then joins them back together as "sum/sum".

One caveat: only N16 and N17 appear in the data provided, so N13:N15 may be empty. If any cell in the range is blank or has no "/", this formula will return an error.

{"formula": "=SUMPRODUCT(--LEFT(N13:N17,FIND(\"/\",N13:N17)-1))&\"/\"&SUMPRODUCT(--MID(N13:N17,FIND(\"/\",N13:N17)+1,9))"}

The header in M3 is "SIGN". The neighbouring columns compare each total in column K against the expected value in K2: L is the difference and N is ABS of the difference. So M4 most likely returns the sign of that same difference, giving 1, -1 or 0.

I assumed K2 is referenced as the absolute `$K$2`. The detected pattern doesn't show whether it is `$K$2` or `K$2`.

{"formula": "=SIGN(K4-$K$2)"}

The neighbouring column BK (Intercept) uses `=INTERCEPT(C5:BE5,C5:BE5)`, so I mirrored that structure for the Slope column in BL.

One caution: that pattern passes the same row as both y and x values. If that is really how BK is written, this slope will always be 1. If the time values are meant to come from row 2, use `=SLOPE(C5:BE5,C$2:BE$2)` instead.

{"formula": "=SLOPE(C5:BE5,C5:BE5)"}

D8 sits in the row labelled "CAGR" in C8, under the YEAR 1 column, with the totals in row 7. So the likely intent is the compound annual growth rate of the portfolio total after one year, measured against the starting total in B7.

The formula divides the Year 1 total by the start total, raises it to the power 1/1 (one year elapsed), and subtracts 1. `$B$7` is absolute so the formula can be filled right, changing the exponent to 1/2, 1/3 and 1/4 for Years 2–4.

{"formula": "=(D7/$B$7)^(1/1)-1"}
